      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability of a specific outcome when rolling two six-sided dice. The outcome we're interested in is rolling a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die.\n\n1. **Determine the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die:**\n   - The numbers less than three on a six-sided die are 1 and 2.\n   - There are 2 favorable outcomes (1 and 2).\n   - The total number of possible outcomes when rolling two six-sided dice is \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\).\n   - Therefore, the probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{first die} < 3) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Determine the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die:**\n   - The numbers greater than three on a six-sided die are 4, 5, and 6.\n   - There are 3 favorable outcomes (4, 5, and 6).\n   - The total number of possible outcomes when rolling two six-sided dice is still 36.\n   - Therefore, the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Determine the combined probability:**\n   - Since the events \"rolling a number less than three on the first die\" and \"rolling a number greater than three on the second die\" are independent, we multiply their probabilities to find the combined probability:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and second die} > 3) = P(\\text{first die} < 3) \\times P(\\text{second die} > 3)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and second die} > 3) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
