      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the outcomes for the first die:**\n   - The first die is a six-sided die.\n   - The numbers on the die are \\(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\).\n   - The numbers less than three are \\(1\\) and \\(2\\).\n   - Therefore, the probability of rolling a number less than three is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{first die} < 3) = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Identify the outcomes for the second die:**\n   - The second die is also a six-sided die.\n   - The numbers on the die are \\(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\).\n   - The numbers greater than three are \\(4, 5, 6\\).\n   - Therefore, the probability of rolling a number greater than three is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the combined probability:**\n   - Since the outcomes of the two dice are independent events, the combined probability is the product of the individual probabilities:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and } \\text{second die} > 3) = P(\\text{first die} < 3) \\times P(\\text{second die} > 3)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{first die} < 3 \\text{ and } \\text{second die} > 3) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
